A||N||0||Bigfieroman||In the spirit of maryjane's post: my bushings||||1||
Z||000000||Bigfieroman||08-09-2001||12:33 PM||||The mechanic was on crack.  After removing the rubber, the front polys fit in perfectly....actually a little too tight for me.  About getting the rubber out, I found a great method of burning that is not nearly as dirty as melting the whole bushing.  At first I tried theogre's method, which is heating the inner sleeve until you can pull it out, and then just cutting the rest out.  I could not cut the bushings out, as after 20 minutes of strugling under the car, I was only able to go from the inner edge of the bushing to the sleeve in a straight cut to the outer edge.  So, I put my heat shields back up,(rubber coolant hose less than 1 inch from bushing, fuel lines about 5 inches away), and torched the sleeve in a one inch area.  After one minute, I could hear it crackling, and it began to ooze out.  I kept going until the rubber would ooze out and catch flame without the aid of heat, and I could no longer blow it out.  Then I just turned off the torch, grabbed a piece of 1.25" dowel and pushed it right out.  It came out so cleanly that almost no sanding/scraping was necessary to clear out the sleeve.  After that it was just a matter of pounding in my frozen poly bushings and buttoning everything back up.  Doing everything took me almost 6 hours, because I had to rig some makeshift platforms for my jacks to stand on among other things.  Infact, I almost had to go to the hospital because I was retarded and didn't drink any water all day, and it was 92 deg, and about 90% humidity.  I just about dehydrated!  I hope this helps some of you out...it only took me about 5 min/bushing once I figured this out!<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
